--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Dependencies
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Plumbline depends on NumPy (BSD-3-Clause) at runtime.

numba (BSD-2-Clause) is an optional extra, used only on platforms with no
compiled wheel. It is not required and not installed by default.

No dependency of this project is under a copyleft licence. That is
deliberate: the software most in need of a correct lossless-JPEG decoder is
commercial medical imaging software and medical device firmware, and a
copyleft dependency would put this library out of their reach.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Not a medical device
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This software decodes medical images. It is NOT a medical device, has not
been submitted to any regulator, and must not be relied upon as the basis of
a diagnosis. Anyone incorporating it into a regulated product is responsible
for their own verification and validation; see CORRECTNESS.md for what this
project does and does not claim, and for the evidence behind those claims.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Test data
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This repository contains no patient data. The conformance corpus is generated
synthetically by the test suite; the eight sample frames under `tests/data/`
are public research data under CC BY, attributed below. A pre-commit hook and
a CI job both reject files that look like medical images, by content rather
than by filename. Enable the hook in your clone with:

    git config core.hooksPath .githooks

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Test frames from the NCI Imaging Data Commons
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`tests/data/idc/` holds eight lossless JPEG frames taken from public
research collections via the NCI Imaging Data Commons. They cover PET and
scanner firmware from outside the region the rest of the corpus came from.

They are bare JPEG frames — the entropy-coded image only. No DICOM tags,
and so nothing identifying a patient, travelled with them.

**These frames have been modified.** Each was extracted from a DICOM file in
the collection named below: the encapsulated JPEG stream was taken out and
every DICOM data element was discarded. The entropy-coded image is unchanged
— not re-encoded, not cropped, not rescaled — but the file you have here is
not the file the collection publishes.

Each is redistributed under the Creative Commons Attribution licence its
collection carries. Cite the collection by its DOI, which is the attribution
those collections ask for:

  nlst  —  CC BY 4.0  ·  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    https://doi.org/10.7937/tcia.hmq8-j677

  tcga_ucec  —  CC BY 3.0  ·  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/
    https://doi.org/10.7937/k9/tcia.2016.gkj0zwac

  upenn_gbm  —  CC BY 4.0  ·  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    https://doi.org/10.7937/tcia.709x-dn49

The scanner behind each frame is recorded below and in the manifest. No
licence requires it. It is here because it is the reason these eight files
exist: they cover scanner firmware the rest of the corpus never sees, and a
decoder bug that only one vendor's encoder can produce is worth being able to
point at.

  nlst        Philips Mx8000 (CT) · SIEMENS Volume Zoom (CT)
  tcga_ucec   GE MEDICAL SYSTEMS Discovery 690 (PT) · Discovery RX (PT)
              SIEMENS SOMATOM Definition AS+ (CT) · Definition Flash (CT)
  upenn_gbm   SIEMENS TrioTim (MR) · SIEMENS Verio (MR)

Full per-file attribution, including checksums, is in
`tests/data/idc/manifest.json`.
